    "Settings.Behaviour.Title": "Behavior",
    "Settings.Behaviour.AlwaysOnTop": "Always on top",
    "Settings.Behaviour.AlwaysOnTop.Hint": "Keeps the dock above other windows — except a window in front that is maximized or fullscreen on the dock's screen: then the dock slides away, and comes back when the pointer reaches the bottom of the screen under it. Turned off, it can be covered like any other window, and holding the pointer against the bottom edge under it brings it to the front until you move away.",
    "Settings.Behaviour.AutoHide": "Hide the dock until needed",
    "Settings.Behaviour.AutoHide.Hint": "The dock slides away, and returns when the pointer reaches the bottom of the screen under it. It does this anyway while a maximized or fullscreen window is in front.",
    "Settings.Behaviour.HideDelay": "Hide delay",
    "Settings.Behaviour.HideDelay.Hint": "How long after the pointer leaves the dock before auto-hide slides it away, or a dock brought to the front goes back behind the window it was under.",
    "Settings.Behaviour.HideDelay.Value": "{0:0} ms",
    "Settings.Behaviour.RevealDelay": "Reveal delay",
    "Settings.Behaviour.RevealDelay.Hint": "How long the pointer must be held against the bottom edge under the dock before a hidden dock returns or a covered one comes to the front, so brushing past does neither.",
    "Settings.Behaviour.RevealDelay.Value": "{0:0} ms",
    "Settings.Behaviour.WindowPreviews": "Show previews of an app's windows",
    "Settings.Behaviour.WindowPreviews.Hint": "Rest the pointer on the icon of an app that is running to see its windows, live, above the dock, as the taskbar shows them. Click one to go to it; its close button or a middle click closes it.",
    "Settings.Behaviour.PreviewDelay": "Preview delay",
    "Settings.Behaviour.PreviewDelay.Hint": "How long the pointer must rest on a running app's icon before its windows are shown, so moving across the dock does not bring them up.",
    "Settings.Behaviour.PreviewDelay.Value": "{0:0} ms",
    "Settings.Behaviour.Handle": "Show a handle while hidden",
    "Settings.Behaviour.Handle.Hint": "A slim bar just above the taskbar marks where the dock is while you cannot see it — slid away, or under other windows. It is only a mark: hold the pointer against the bottom edge under the dock to bring it up. It shows the colors behind it reversed, so it stands out on any background, and screenshots and recordings leave it out. It is not shown over anything fullscreen, such as a video or a game.",
    "Settings.Behaviour.HandleMatchesDock": "As wide as the dock",
    "Settings.Behaviour.HandleMatchesDock.Hint": "Follows the dock as icons come and go. Turn off to give the handle a width of its own.",
    "Settings.Behaviour.HandleWidth": "Handle width",
    "Settings.Behaviour.HandleWidth.Value": "{0:0} px",
    "Settings.Behaviour.Reset": "Reset Behavior to defaults",

    "Settings.Hotkeys.Title": "Hotkeys",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Hint": "Keys that reach the dock from anywhere in Windows. Click a box, then press the keys: the Windows key, Ctrl or Alt, with one other key. Backspace, or the × in the box, empties it, and Esc puts back what it held. Keys that Windows keeps for itself never reach the box.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Take": "Use the dock from the keyboard",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Take.Hint": "Brings the dock up with an item held up. Left and Right move along it, Home and End go to the ends, a letter goes to the next item whose name starts with it, and 1 to 9 go to the item in that place. Enter or Space opens the item held up, the menu key or Shift+F10 opens its menu, and Esc — or these keys again — goes back to where you were.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.ShowHide": "Hide or show the dock",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.ShowHide.Hint": "The same as Hide dock on the tray icon's menu.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Settings": "Open the dock's settings",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Settings.Hint": "Opens this window, or brings it to the front if it is open. The same as Settings on the tray icon's menu.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Numbers": "Show item numbers on Win+Ctrl",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Numbers.Hint": "Holding the Windows key and Ctrl down puts a number on each item that quick launch can open: the number on its key. Not while an app on the Exclusions page is in front.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Reveal": "Reveal the dock on Win+Ctrl",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Reveal.Hint": "Holding the Windows key and Ctrl down brings a hidden dock up until you let go, so the item numbers can be seen. Not while an app on the Exclusions page is in front.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Places": "Open an item",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Places.Hint": "Opens the item in that place on the dock, as a click on it does, counting from the left without the separators. To begin with, the Windows key and Ctrl with the number on the numeric keypad, which needs Num Lock on: Windows uses the number row with the Windows key for its taskbar. Without a keypad, use the dock from the keyboard, press the number, then Enter. The second keys are not set to begin with; the Windows key, Ctrl and Alt with a number on the number row is a combination Windows leaves free.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.QuickLaunch": "Enable quick launch",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.QuickLaunch.Hint": "The keys below open the items, and holding the Windows key and Ctrl shows each item's number on the dock. Turned off, the dock lets go of them, and they are free for other programs to use. Each item can be turned off by itself as well.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Place": "Item {0}",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Place.Hint": "Turned off, the dock lets go of this item's keys, and they are free for other programs to use.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Primary": "First key",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Secondary": "Second key",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Place.Secondary": "Item {0}, second key",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.None": "None",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Clear": "Clear",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Reset": "Reset Hotkeys to defaults",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Taken": "Another program already has these keys, so they do nothing here. Choose others.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Shared": "These keys are already set for “{0}”, which keeps them.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.AltGr": "On many keyboards Ctrl+Alt is AltGr, so these keys can stop a character from being typed. With the Windows key as well, they would not.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Programs": "Programs may use these keys themselves, and the dock takes them from all of them. A combination with the Windows key is less likely to be one.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.NeedsModifier": "Hold the Windows key, Ctrl or Alt as well, so the keys are not taken from typing.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Reserved": "Windows keeps F12 for debuggers, so it cannot be used.",
    "Settings.Hotkeys.Windows": "Windows uses these keys in every window, to switch between windows and to close them.",

    "Settings.Exclusions.Title": "Excluded apps",
    "Settings.Exclusions.Hint": "The dock will not come up over these apps, or take their keys. While one of them is in front and fills the screen the dock is on — fullscreen or borderless — holding the pointer against the bottom edge does nothing: a hidden dock stays hidden, a covered one stays covered, and no handle is shown. While one of them is in front at all, whatever its size and whichever screen it is on, the dock's hotkeys are turned off, so those keys reach the app. For games that scroll when the pointer reaches the edge of the screen, or that use the same keys as the dock.",
    "Settings.Exclusions.Empty": "No apps yet. Add one that is open now, browse for its program, or scan for games or apps.",
